== 3.7 Chapter 3 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [“The bus arrived, and the queue dispersed in ninety seconds.” The passage implies that],
  [the queue was orderly],
  [the bus was crowded],
  [the bus was early],
  [the passengers had been waiting a long time]
)

#mcq(2, [An option that needs a fact the passage never gives is],
  [a reversal],
  [an assumption],
  [an inference],
  [a detail]
)

#mcq(3, [“He was firm: he refused every extension and reminded them twice that the deadline had been published.” The word *firm* here is],
  [capable of approval, and withdrawn by the rest of the passage],
  [a synonym for cruel],
  [neutral],
  [wholly approving]
)

#mcq(4, [Tone is decided by],
  [the subject of the passage],
  [the number of examples],
  [the words the writer chooses],
  [the length of the sentences]
)

#mcq(5, [“The ministry has at last published the figures.” The words *at last* suggest that the writer thinks the publication was],
  [generous],
  [prompt],
  [overdue],
  [unnecessary]
)

#mcq(6, [Irony is a figure in which the writer],
  [compares two unlike things],
  [exaggerates for effect],
  [says the opposite of what is meant],
  [gives a thing human qualities]
)

#mcq(7, [“A triumph of presentation: four colours, twelve charts, and not one comparable number.” The clause after the colon],
  [withdraws the praise],
  [supports the praise],
  [repeats the praise],
  [explains the method]
)

#mcq(8, [“The residents have stopped waiting.” The sentence suggests],
  [an increase in waiting],
  [patience],
  [a new committee],
  [loss of confidence in the council]
)

#mcq(9, [The attitude of a writer towards a person is best found by],
  [collecting the words used to describe that person],
  [noticing the tense],
  [reading the title],
  [counting the mentions]
)

#mcq(10, [“It is a fence with traffic on it.” The image suggests],
  [speed],
  [danger],
  [separation],
  [connection]
)

#mcq(11, [A figure of speech in an exam question is usually tested by asking],
  [whether it is a cliché],
  [its name],
  [its origin],
  [what it means and what it is doing]
)

#mcq(12, [“The scheme, so-called, has produced three reports and no water.” The words *so-called* show that the writer],
  [rejects the name],
  [accepts the name],
  [is neutral about the name],
  [has forgotten the name]
)

#mcq(13, [Which sentence is most likely to be ironic?],
  [The bridge cost two billion naira.],
  [The road was closed for repairs.],
  [The council has produced a plan, eleven years after the first promise.],
  [The traders left at noon.]
)

#mcq(14, [An inference question's wrong options are usually],
  [summaries],
  [quotations],
  [assumptions the passage never commits itself to],
  [statements that contradict the passage]
)

#mcq(15, [“Appears to have been caused by the rain” is the language of],
  [certainty],
  [irony],
  [alarm],
  [caution]
)

#mcq(16, [The sentence “He did not listen” is best described as],
  [a judgement presented as a fact],
  [a quotation],
  [a stated fact],
  [an assumption]
)

#mcq(17, [A passage that describes a corrupt official in flat, orderly sentences is most likely],
  [alarmed],
  [controlled and damning],
  [approving],
  [celebratory]
)

#mcq(18, [“The figures are a triumph of presentation.” The phrase is sarcastic because],
  [the praise is given to the wrong quality],
  [figures are boring],
  [triumphs are rare],
  [presentation is unimportant]
)

#mcq(19, [To say that a tone option is *supported* means],
  [it is the commonest tone],
  [it is mentioned in the passage],
  [a word or sentence in the passage can be pointed at in its favour],
  [the writer says it directly]
)

#mcq(20, [“The committee toured the affected streets once.” The word *once* suggests],
  [thoroughness],
  [the minimum of effort],
  [generosity],
  [efficiency]
)

#mcq(21, [A candidate who answers a tone question by matching the tone to the subject is],
  [assuming that bad subjects make angry writers],
  [checking the sentence],
  [using the reference test],
  [applying the one-step rule]
)

#mcq(22, [“A corridor of prosperity” is an example of],
  [hyperbole],
  [personification],
  [irony],
  [metaphor]
)

#mcq(23, [The best way to answer “the writer's attitude towards the residents is…” is to],
  [count how often they appear],
  [read the last sentence only],
  [find the words that describe them],
  [look at the title]
)

#mcq(24, [An implied conclusion must be],
  [forced by the passage's words],
  [the writer's opinion],
  [true in the world],
  [stated in the passage]
)

#mcq(25, [“The report recommends that a further report be commissioned.” The sentence is],
  [a criticism by example],
  [a neutral fact],
  [a compliment],
  [an inference]
)

#mcq(26, [Which pair of tone words is most likely to appear as the last two options on a question?],
  [happy / joyful],
  [critical / cautious],
  [long / short],
  [angry / furious]
)

#mcq(27, [“As usual, the council responded with a committee.” The words *as usual* imply],
  [that the practice is a habit],
  [approval of the practice],
  [that the council is generous],
  [that committees are efficient]
)

#mcq(28, [An inference that takes two steps is],
  [still safe],
  [no longer supported by the passage],
  [a detail],
  [the main idea]
)

#mcq(29, [“Officials say the delay was caused by the need for accuracy.” The writer reports the excuse],
  [and contradicts it directly],
  [and repeats it twice],
  [without endorsing it],
  [and endorses it]
)

#mcq(30, [The difference between tone and attitude is that attitude],
  [has a target — a person, group or thing],
  [is about the whole passage],
  [is stated by the writer],
  [is always negative]
)

#mcq(31, [“A triumph of presentation” is ironic because the writer],
  [values the contents more than the packaging],
  [dislikes presentations],
  [does not like triumph],
  [is writing about sport]
)

#mcq(32, [A figure of speech is best explained in an exam by],
  [giving its origin],
  [saying what is compared with what, and what that says],
  [saying whether it is good],
  [naming it]
)

#mcq(33, [Which is the strongest evidence that a writer is critical?],
  [a long passage],
  [a quotation from an official],
  [a date],
  [a judgement delivered as a fact]
)

#mcq(34, [“The residents, who have rebuilt their drainage twice, have stopped waiting.” The clause about the drainage is there to],
  [show the residents' skill],
  [criticise the residents],
  [contrast the residents' effort with the council's inaction],
  [explain the flood]
)

#mcq(35, [A candidate who cannot point at a word supporting an attitude option should],
  [choose it anyway],
  [check the title],
  [choose the longest option],
  [discard it]
)

#mcq(36, [“He reminded them, twice, that the deadline had been published.” The words *twice* suggest],
  [forgetfulness on his part],
  [that he was insistent, perhaps excessively],
  [generosity],
  [uncertainty]
)

#mcq(37, [The commonest wrong option on an inference question is],
  [a contradiction of the passage],
  [a quotation],
  [a summary],
  [a sensible statement the passage never commits itself to]
)

#mcq(38, [“The plan, such as it is, will be published next year.” The phrase *such as it is* signals],
  [neutrality],
  [approval],
  [contempt],
  [confusion]
)

#mcq(39, [Tone questions are best answered],
  [before reading the passage],
  [from the title],
  [by counting adjectives],
  [after finding the verdict sentence]
)

#mcq(40, [The safest reading of a sentence whose praise is contradicted by its own second half is],
  [hyperbole],
  [literal praise],
  [personification],
  [irony]
)

